A||||17||Gokart||Buffer||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||03-12-2000||06:36 AM||||My birthday is coming up and my wife is going to get me buffer! Can anyone recommend me one that works well that costs between $50.00 and $100.00? Also, what product works well with the buffer at removing scratches? From removing/installing the battery so many times during the winter I now have scratches from leaning on the car from my zipper on the jacket.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gokart||03-12-2000||09:14 PM||||Nobody has a buffer that their happy with? ||||reg||5
Z||000002||Cooter||03-12-2000||09:31 PM||||I have a Craftsman orbital buffer from sears that I like. I cant remember how much it costed, but I believe that $100 should cover it. It is good for polishing, but not buffing out big paint problems like overspray, dryspray, wet sanded paint etc... If you have to do "heavy duty" buffing, an electric or (my favorite) air powered non-orbital buffer is preferred.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||03-12-2000||11:43 PM||||I've got a WEN orbital, same as cooter, it works good for polishing, I also use it to remove wax before it is completely dry. I think most orditals are about the same and the same in $$ it's a 10", 1/2 hp,  <BR>3000 orbits per. min. Got it at Grand Auto about three years ago and it still works like new, it was under $100.00||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||03-13-2000||10:22 AM||||gokart,<P>Are you speaking of an orbital or a high speed?  I worked as a detailer for several years and have used everything from Makita to Wen.  I would gladly give you some opinions of each, but need to know what type you are referring to.  I would imagine from the price range you are talking about a high speed.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||5
Z||000005||Gokart||03-13-2000||07:39 PM||||Thanks guys. William, what do you suggest? I need a buffer that will help fill in some small scartches. Which tool? What product works good that doesn't remove the clear coat?||||reg||5
Z||000006||william_thorniii||03-13-2000||08:10 PM||||gokart,<P>Orbitals only reduce time to do a wax job in my opinion.  If all you need is something to make waxing quicker, then you can go with a $30-$40 Wen from Auto Zone (or similar).  Depending on the scratches a wax job might or might not do what you need it to.  <P>A high speed buffer is a much more valuable tool in my opinion.  A high speed is essentially a big rotary sander.  It consists basically of a drill motor and a right angle spindle.  Also these are useful for other sanding applications.  <P>Like I said before, an orbital only helps apply and take off wax.  You could do both of those regardless if you have one or not.  This is precisely why I would purchase a high speed buffer if I had to choose.  <P>A high speed buffer actually burns the top layer of your cc or paint off and in doing so remelts some into the grooves.  Although this sounds scary, if done properly it is one of the few things that can actually repair paint to a better than new finish.  <P>The process is very easy and I will be happy to provide you with more information if this is what you feel is best for you.  Please note that you must use extreme care when using a high-speed buffer.  Mistakes can be costly in the form of burn marks and broken side moldings if you are not careful.  If you are careful and good with a sander they are an invaluable tool.  Furthermore when used properly there is less danger to your paint than there is in washing it with a sponge or leaning up against it.<P>William T. Thorn, III  ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Gokart||03-13-2000||08:27 PM||||Thanks alot Will! High speed buffer it is! Doubt that I could find one at Walmart. Maybe Sears? Which ones best in your opinion. What product would you recommend to use with it to help fill in scratches?||||reg||14
Z||000008||87SC/GT||03-13-2000||09:11 PM||||Here's my 2 cents. Get a little more money together and buy a professional quality buffer. It will cost you about 200.00 for a good quality variable speed unit. This is one of those lifetime tool investments unless you want to buy a 100.00 buffer every couple of years. The Milwaukee is an excellent choice in this price range.The 50-100.00 buffers are good if you understand their limits. I, unfortunately  had to burn a few up to find out what those limits were. Good Luck<p>[This message has been edited by 87SC/GT (edited 03-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||doughboySE||03-13-2000||10:35 PM||||hey guys i got a 10 inch random orbit polisher/waxer for xmas<BR>its a 120V<BR>2600 OPM it weighs 5 lbs <BR>WHAT YOU THINK? IS THIS A GOOD POLISHER? ANY TRICKS TO USING IT OTHER THEN DO ALL THE NEIGHBOURS CARS BEFORE I TRY MY OWN? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||03-15-2000||01:24 PM||||Gokart,<P>In your price range the Sears seems to be the best value.  I personally own one but it is definitely not a professional unit.  The biggest problem with this model is that the speed is only trigger sensative.  I have not had any problems with the motor and have used it extensively.  On higher end units (like the Milwaukee) an additional knob is supplied for this purpose.  It isn't a huge deal, but my trigger finger sure does get sore running at a partially depressed postition for a long time.   <P>There are millions of products out there.  I reccomend finding a local detailer and asking if they will asses your paint.  Ask if you can buy the products that they would use on a finish like yours.  I am most familiar with Zep products.  Beware though, some of these products can get VERY expensive.  <BR> <BR>doughboySE<P>The good thing about orbitals is that there are no tricks.  The only reccomendation that I have is to spread the wax around first by hand before using the orbital.  Otherwise you will be cleaning speckles of wax and buffing compound off your windshield and wipers for quite a while.  You can't do any damage using an orbital as long as the pad is the only part that touches the paint. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000011||doughboySE||03-15-2000||02:51 PM||||thanx pal i appriciate that i was thinking the same thing thanx again||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||03-15-2000||03:05 PM||||doughboy SE,<P>Anytime you need advice from an automotive detailer... feel free to look me up.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000013||Old Lar||03-15-2000||03:09 PM||||I used a Sears grinder/buffer, heavy duty.  The grinder really did a nice job at cleaning up metal for body repair.<P>But as a polisher, I burnt the paint several times.  It takes some expertise to use the heavy duty things as a polishing buffer.  You cannot stay in one place too long.<P>I use an inexpensive Black and Decker random orbital polisher to put on cleaners, polishes or waxes,(new foam bonnet for each step) but use the tried and true all cotton cloth to remove and buff the car to a high shine.  Elbow grease wins out.<P>Since a Fiero is small, you can do a lot in one or two hours.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 03-15-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000014||william_thorniii||03-15-2000||04:16 PM||||Old Lar,<P>Rule #1:  Use a good quality buffing head (wool is preferred in most normal instances)<P>Rule #2:  Keep moving.<P>Rule #3:  Do NOT apply much if any downward pressure to the buffer.  The weight of the tool is usually enough to accomplish light duty jobs.  <P>Please note that those are only a partial list... I only went that far because they cover some of the most common misconceptions about buffing paint. <P>To anyone even considering doing a high-speed buff, you should really get in contact with someone that knows what they are doing first.  It is easy, but there are a few rules one should adhere by when using a high speed sanding tool to restore one's paint.  Again, anyone that is considering doing this should contact an experienced detailer before attempting your first detail.   <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000015||Shiner||03-15-2000||05:26 PM||||I have the same Craftsman buffer that cooter has.  It's under $100, and well worth it.  It really works great for polishing compound or wax.  So far it has been a great little tool.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Gokart||03-15-2000||07:39 PM||||Thanks alot Will for time spent on posts above! Will talk to an expert or two before using. I decided I'm going to spend a little more and get a Milwaukee heavy-duty 7" polisher (model 5540). It varies in speed from 0 to 2800 rpm!||||reg||14
Z||000017||william_thorniii||03-16-2000||11:13 AM||||Gokart,<P>Good choice in buffer.  That will be useful in any sanding application as well.  <P>Anytime you need additional help/advice let me know.  Even off-forum your questions are welcome.  Since I can't look at your paint it would be best for you to find some local detailers.  <P>That can be a trick in and of itself.  Your best bet is to find a car wash or dealership that does many full details.  You can probably find them by calling dealerships first and asking who does their details.  Most dealership details are done at night so that they can be on the lot and shiny during the day.  The guys doing the detail will be able to make the best reccomendations.  Also make sure that you tell them that you want products that DO NOT require the use of a foam pad.  The foam doesn't dissipate the heat as well as wool and therefore you run a much higher risk of burnspots.   <P><BR>I'll leave you with a few tips...<P>Have Fun,<BR>keep the speeds slow at first<BR>and stay away from any non uniform surface (i.e. don't try to get right up against body side moldings, drip rails, windshield wipers etc.) at high speeds,<BR>wrap the cord around your elbow and shoulder so it doesn't drag across the paint,<BR>never wash, buff or wax in the sun (difficult in AZ),<BR>don't try to buff and polish in one step,<BR>put a little dab of buff compound on finger tips to determine grit before using (compounds for bad paint are filled with abrasives...)<P>That's about all that I can think of... good luck and your arms will probably be tired afterwards.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
